WEBVTT

00:00:00.004 --> 00:00:04.357
Now let's translate business rules into relational decisions.

00:00:04.357 --> 00:00:09.772
If every appointment belongs to exactly one patient, appointment needs a non-null patient foreign key.

00:00:09.772 --> 00:00:15.162
A patient may exist before any appointment is scheduled, so the patient table does not require a child

00:00:15.162 --> 00:00:19.037
appointment row. The same reasoning applies to provider.

00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:25.017
An appointment may have zero or many service lines, so those optional child rows belong in appointment

00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:25.762
service.

00:00:25.762 --> 00:00:31.442
A service can appear in many appointments, which confirms that appointment and service form a many-to-many

00:00:31.442 --> 00:00:32.532
relationship.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:39.357
The key habit is to read the business sentence first, then decide where the foreign key belongs and whether

00:00:39.357 --> 00:00:41.887
that relationship is required or optional.

00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:44.782
Keys are not merely technical identifiers.

00:00:44.782 --> 00:00:48.992
A primary key protects row identity, a unique constraint protects a business rule,

00:00:48.992 --> 00:00:51.762
and a foreign key protects a relationship.

00:00:51.762 --> 00:00:54.307
In production systems, surrogate keys are often convenient,

00:00:54.307 --> 00:00:58.960
but important natural identifiers may still need their own uniqueness rule.
